  "algorithm_changes": [
    "Spelling: lazily shared compact character trie over eligible lexicon; first-character filter preserved; banded radius-two Levenshtein traversal reuses depth rows; exact distance/frequency/lexical ranking.",
    "Embedding: accumulate observed counts sparsely with dense fallback at quarter occupancy; deterministic sorted CSR PPMI avoids dense vocabulary-square matrices for sparse truncated problems.",
    "Large-space randomized factorization applies both M transpose and M with reorthogonalization; previous assumption that smoothed PPMI is symmetric was false. Fixed deterministic Rademacher projection; thin dense QR/SVD retained.",
    "Small spaces and full-rank requests preserve the exact dense factorization path; 128-vocabulary model vectors match baseline bit for bit.",
    "Drop token sequences before factorization to shorten peak ownership. No dependencies, public APIs, serialization contracts or caps changed."
  ],
  "regressions": [
    "Exact spelling parity against previous full-scan oracle on real misspellings, output limits, Unicode, insertion/deletion/substitution/transposition dictionary mutations, radius/length boundaries and frequency/lexical ties.",
    "Sparse PPMI and forward/transpose products match dense equations with isolated zero rows and asymmetric smoothed marginals.",
    "Adaptive sparse-to-dense promotion verified at threshold-1, threshold, threshold+1 with repeated counts.",
    "Nonsymmetric nilpotent rank-six case verifies transpose power steps, top singular values and relative reconstructed Gram residual <1e-10 against exact dense SVD.",
    "Large sparse corpus reproduces all vectors exactly on repeated training and preserves separated topic neighbors/cosines.",
    "Document edit/addition preserves learned word vectors and atomically refreshes dense/scene/lexical retrieval."
  ],
  "limits": [
    "Synthetic bounded corpora and one machine; no claim about natural-language retrieval quality outside regression fixtures, all platforms or generic embedding models.",
    "512-vocabulary dim100 build is about9% slower; 1000 mixed-context timing is approximately unchanged, despite lower memory. Reorthogonalization intentionally adds numerical stability cost.",
    "First spelling call builds a shared index: about1.09MB retained requested bytes and2MiB peak extra; subsequent lookup timings exclude this one-time construction.",
    "Sparse solver remains approximate for large truncated spaces and may reorder neighbors versus the previous mathematically incorrect symmetric power iteration; same-build determinism is verified, cross-platform bitwise equality is not claimed.",
    "Dense fallback remains for high occupancy, small vocabularies and near-full-rank requests; no universal subquadratic bound is claimed.",
    "Tracked memory is logical Rust allocation, not full process RSS; short sample physical footprints are supplementary observations only.",
